<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jag Mandir</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jag Mandir is a palace on an island in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-pichola">Lake Pichola</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udaipur">Udaipur</a>, built mainly under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharana">Maharana</a> Karan Singh II in the 1620s and completed by Jagat Singh I, after whom it is named. The Mughal prince Khurram, the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan">Shah Jahan</a>, took refuge there during his rebellion against his father in 1623–1624, and in 1857 Maharana Swarup Singh sheltered European refugees on the island.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jag-mandir.jpg" alt="Jag Mandir" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: No machine-readable author provided. Philbrest assumed (based on copyright claims). · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:JagMandir.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
